            DIGITAL IN WINNING TEAM TO PROVIDE MESSAGING
         BACKBONE FOR UNITED KINGDOM NATIONAL HEALTH SERVICE


MAYNARD, Mass. -- October 12, 1994 -- Digital is part of the team 
awarded a framework contract by the United Kingdom's National Health 
Service (NHS) to provide a message handling service that could 
eventually be worth $70-140 million.

     Syntegra, the systems integration arm of British Telecom (BT), 
is leading the winning team and Digital is providing the core 
messaging technology for a pilot system. If fully deployed, it will 
be developed into one of the largest private messaging systems in 
the world, with up to 500,000 users.

     As part of the project, Digital has secured a pilot contract, 
worth over $700,000, for two Digital 3000 Model 800s Server systems, 
based on Alpha AXP technology, running Digital's MAILbus 400 and DEC 
X.500 Directory Services electronic mail software to manage the 
network. The full backbone network could eventually grow to ten 
Digital 2100 Server systems, each capable of processing one million 
messages a day.

     Part of a seven-year investment program by the NHS, Syntegra's 
proposed message handling service will link thousands of medical and 
dental practices, and hundreds of hospitals and Health Authorities 
across the United Kingdom into one nation-wide network.

     Nancy Strecker, vice president, Digital Software Products, 
commented, "This could become the largest messaging service of its 
kind in the world, with between 250,000 and 500,000 anticipated 
users. Providing the messaging integration framework for this 
project demonstrates that Digital has the best client/server 
technology on the market."

     According to Tom Sackville, Parliamentary Under Secretary of 
State in the United Kingdom, "The system will make substantial 
savings for patient care and speed up the secure transfer of all 
sorts of data around the NHS." The message handling service is a 
critical part of the NHS-wide networking infrastructure which will 
support electronic communications throughout the NHS, including the
instant distribution of emergency medical messages.

     Dennis Shaugnhessy, Syntegra's account manager for the NHS 
said, "Syntegra has recognized the increasing need for partnership 
and risk-sharing with its customers. We have the technical and 
business expertise, and the financial resources, to take on this 
type of project." Syntegra claims the service could eventually 
handle more than one million messages a day and will use the most
up-to-date messaging technology -- Digital's MAILbus 400 -- which is 
soon to be used for BT's own public messaging service.

     Syntegra, the systems integration business of BT, delivers 
large-scale solutions which enable customers to achieve business 
change through the application of technology. Syntegra is one of 
the largest and most successful U.K.-owned systems integration 
businesses with revenues of around $350 million. Based in Fleet in 
the U.K., Syntegra employs more than 1500 people.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
