                DIGITAL AND MICROMALL SIGN AGREEMENT
       TO PROVIDE ELECTRONIC SHOPPING ON INTERACTIVE NETWORKS


          Lands' End First Merchant To Sign Up For Expanded
                        Market Opportunities


MAYNARD, Mass. -- October 25, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
and MicroMall Inc., today announced an agreement to build at-home 
shopping environments that provide retail, travel and advertising 
services on TV systems in major worldwide markets served by 
interactive networks.  Under terms of the non-exclusive agreement, 
MicroMalls electronic mall expertise will be matched with Digitals 
video server technology and Digital Media Studio resources, 
providing retailers with complete development and distribution 
services for interactive merchandising programs.

     "Digital and MicroMall are taking the right approach to at-home 
interactive shopping by involving merchants in what we do best -- 
merchandising," said Mike Atkin, vice president of marketing, Lands' 
End, Dodgeville, Wis.  "This will ensure that consumers get what we 
believe they want: shopper-friendly products created and presented 
by merchants, not programmers."  Lands' End, a major worldwide 
cataloger, is the first retailer to sign with MicroMall's 
Interactive Merchants' Association and to participate in the content 
creation and distribution capabilities of the Digital Media Studio.

     According to Charles Spong, president of MicroMall, Inc., 
"Retailers are constantly seeking new distribution channels that can 
expand their reach while lowering the cost-of-sales.  The Digital 
and MicroMall agreement will help retailers to quickly reach more 
customers.  It represents the first unified approach enabling 
retailers to distribute their goods and services over multiple 
interactive networks with production, promotion, merchandising, 
usage tracking, transaction management and billing handled by one 
point of contact."

     "Digital's video server is being used in more interactive 
trials and deployments than any other server technology because it 
has the power and flexibility needed for large-scale customer 
usage," said Phil Corman, corporate manager of interactive media at 
Digital Equipment Corporation.  "By teaming with MicroMall, we're 
laying the groundwork that will enable retailers who sign on now to 
immediately benefit from the expanded markets available through
wide-reaching home-shopping deployments."

     Corman added that Digital's Media Studio will provide a 
comprehensive solution for retailers seeking to easily create 
interactive electronic "store-front" programs.  Retailers involved 
now will welcome their first electronic shoppers in deployments 
scheduled for June 1995.

     "I believe retailers will respond favorably to this turnkey 
solution that operates on many different platforms," said Atkin.  
"With MicroMall and Digital, Lands' End can create a single 'sales 
presentation' running on multiple interactive networks, 
simultaneously."

     Digitals video server technology is being adopted for trials 
and deployments by major telephone companies including U S West's 
pilot project in Omaha, Nebraska; a NYNEX deployment in Rhode 
Island; Telstra/Telecom Australia's first pilot program, and several 
other undisclosed partners.  The companys Digital Media Center, 
located in Tarrytown, New York, streamlines the design and
development of interactive video applications and services with a 
full range of program creation, conversion, compression and 
distribution services.

     Incorporated in 1990, MicroMall is a majority-owned subsidiary 
of Microware Systems Corp. of Des Moines, Iowa.  MicroMalls 
electronic mall framework, including its proprietary graphic and 
data processing technologies, enables consumers to find and buy 
products and services quickly and easily.  The company also provides 
interactive retail-oriented services ranging from product
presentation to order processing through its Interactive Merchants 
Association.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry focused solutions from business partners, help
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
